        <div id="first-rule" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The First Rule: What Never Comes Indoors
          </h2>

          <p>
            Before talking about which heater to buy, it is worth being blunt about what kills people in winter outages. Every year, families bring a charcoal grill, a camp stove, a patio heater or a portable generator into a garage, basement or living room to stay warm, and every year some of them do not wake up. Carbon monoxide from those sources builds to lethal levels in minutes, with no smell, and it can seep into a house from an attached garage even with the big door open.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Rule</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Never use a grill, camp stove, oven, patio heater or generator for indoor heat.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Not in the house, not in the basement, not in an attached garage with the door open, not in a tent or camper. Generators belong at least 20 feet from the house with the exhaust pointed away from doors and windows. Indoor heat comes only from heaters certified for indoor use, with a carbon monoxide alarm running.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Using a gas oven or range burners to heat a kitchen belongs on the same list. Kitchen appliances are not designed to run for hours as space heaters, and an oven left open and on can produce carbon monoxide and starve a closed room of oxygen. The same goes for outdoor propane heaters, including tabletop patio units and torpedo-style forced-air heaters built for job sites. They are not designed for living spaces.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you plan to run a generator during an outage, read our guide to <a href="/where-to-place-a-generator-outside/">where to place a generator outside</a> before the first storm. The rest of this guide covers what you can safely use indoors, from propane heaters with oxygen depletion sensors to flame-free options that are safe while you sleep.
          </p>
        </div>

