          <p>
            Portable radiant propane heaters are the best all-round choice for most homes. They are cheap, widely sold, certified for indoor use in most of the US, and the indoor-rated models carry an oxygen depletion sensor and a tip-over switch. They also store well: an unopened propane cylinder does not go stale the way gasoline does.
          </p>

          <p>
            Vent-free gas heaters make sense if you already have propane or natural gas plumbed to the house and want permanent backup heat. Kerosene heaters deliver lots of heat cheaply but demand the most care. Catalytic heaters stretch fuel the furthest but put out the least heat. Electric options on a battery are the only truly combustion-free choice, and they are the safest for overnight use. For a deeper look at the electric side, see our <a href="/best-electric-space-heater/">best electric space heaters guide</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever you choose, look for indoor certification on the label. Many propane heaters sold for patios, garages and job sites look similar to indoor models but lack an ODS. If the box or manual says outdoor use only, or for ventilated construction areas, it does not belong in your living room.
          </p>
