        <div id="propane-safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Is a Propane Heater Safe Indoors?
          </h2>

          <p>
            An indoor-rated propane heater can be used safely indoors, but only with the right model, the right setup and a carbon monoxide alarm. The heaters that qualify carry an oxygen depletion sensor, which shuts off the gas if the oxygen level in the room drops, and a tip-over switch, which shuts it off if the heater is knocked over. The Mr. Heater Buddy family is the most common example, and the DEWALT DXH12B, made by the same company, is another.
          </p>

          <p>
            The ODS is often misunderstood. It is a pilot-based sensor that reacts to falling oxygen. It does not measure carbon monoxide, and it cannot detect CO entering from somewhere else, like an attached garage. That is why the manuals for these heaters require fresh-air ventilation and why a separate CO alarm is mandatory. Crack a window an inch or two, ideally on a different wall from the heater, so fresh air can replace what the burner uses.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clearance is the other big rule. Keep at least three feet between the heater and anything that can burn: curtains, bedding, sofas, paper, clothing drying on a chair. Put the heater on a hard, level surface where it will not be kicked or knocked by pets or children. Never leave it running while you sleep, and never leave it unattended. Turn it off before bed and use blankets or a battery-powered electric blanket overnight.
          </p>

          <p>
            A few more practical points. Swap 1 lb cylinders outdoors, and if you run a 20 lb tank through a hose, keep the tank outside or in a well-ventilated space and use the inline filter the manufacturer specifies to protect the regulator. At higher elevations, the ODS may shut the heater off early, because the air holds less oxygen. And check local rules: some jurisdictions, including Massachusetts and Canada for certain models, restrict indoor propane heaters. Follow the label for where you live.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Propane Runtime Math</p>
