        <div id="carbon-monoxide" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Carbon Monoxide: The Part You Cannot Skip
          </h2>

          <p>
            Carbon monoxide is produced whenever fuel burns incompletely. A well-tuned indoor propane heater produces very little, but very little adds up in a closed room over hours, and a heater that is dirty, low on oxygen or running on a bad regulator can produce much more. Kerosene heaters, especially with a poorly trimmed wick, can produce more still. The gas has no color, smell or taste.
          </p>

          <p>
            Early CO poisoning feels like the flu: headache, dizziness, nausea, weakness and confusion. Several people in a house feeling sick at once, especially if they feel better after leaving, is a classic warning sign. Sleeping people and pets may show no symptoms before it is too late. If an alarm sounds or anyone has symptoms, turn off the heater if it is safe, get everyone outside into fresh air immediately and call 911.
          </p>

          <p>
            The fix is cheap. Put a carbon monoxide alarm with battery backup, or a fully battery-powered alarm, in the room with the heater and another outside sleeping areas. A plug-in alarm without battery backup is useless in a power outage. Test alarms when you set up your outage kit, replace backup batteries yearly, and replace the whole alarm when it reaches its end-of-life date, typically seven to ten years.
          </p>

          <p>
            Carbon monoxide can also come from outside the room. A generator running too close to a window, a car warming up in an attached garage or a neighbor's generator near your wall can all push CO indoors. An alarm catches all of these. Our <a href="/how-to-use-a-portable-generator/">guide to using a portable generator safely</a> covers the placement rules that keep generator exhaust out of the house.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="kerosene-vent-free" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Kerosene and Vent-Free Gas: When They Make Sense
          </h2>

          <p>
            Kerosene heaters earn their place in rural homes with long outages. A 23,800 BTU convection heater can warm a large room for many hours on a single tank, kerosene stores for a season, and the heater needs no electricity. But kerosene asks more of you than propane does. Indoor kerosene heaters do not have oxygen depletion sensors, they emit carbon monoxide, nitrogen dioxide and sulfur dioxide, and they smell at startup and shutdown.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you choose kerosene, follow the rules without exception. Use only clear 1-K grade kerosene, never gasoline, diesel, camp fuel or dyed kerosene, which can cause flare-ups and heavy emissions. Refuel outdoors and only after the heater has cooled. Keep the wick clean and set at the correct height. Ventilate the room, keep the heater away from foot traffic and children, never move it while lit and never run it overnight. Check local rules first, because some cities ban kerosene heaters in homes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Vent-free gas heaters are a better fit for homes with propane or natural gas already on site. Once installed, they light with a battery igniter, run on a thermostat and need no hauling of fuel. The downside is that they vent everything into the room, including a surprising amount of water vapor that can cause condensation. Some states, including California, and many local codes prohibit them in homes, and fuel gas codes restrict them in bedrooms and bathrooms. Installation is a job for a licensed gas fitter.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most suburban households, neither kerosene nor vent-free gas is the first choice. A Buddy-style propane heater with a 20 lb tank offers most of the benefits with simpler storage and better built-in safeguards. Kerosene and vent-free heaters are worth it where outages are long and frequent and the household will commit to the safety routine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="power-station-heat" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Power-Station-Friendly Heat and Electric Blankets
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you already own a portable power station, or are thinking about one for outages, it changes the heating question. A power station cannot run a 1,500W space heater for long: a 1,024Wh unit gives about 35 minutes on high. But it can run an electric blanket all night, recharge heated vests and hand warmers many times, and keep the essentials running alongside. That makes it the perfect overnight partner to a propane heater used during the day.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Load</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical draw</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">1,024Wh station</th>
