                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">300-800W running</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1 to 3 hr</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2 to 5 hr</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The furnace row deserves a note. A gas furnace needs electricity for its blower and controls, and in some homes an electrician can install an inlet or transfer switch that lets a power station or generator run the furnace. That gives whole-house heat from your normal fuel. A small power station only runs it for a few hours, which is why larger stations and generators are the usual choice for this; see our guide to <a href="/can-a-generator-power-a-furnace-refrigerator-and-sump-pump/">powering a furnace, fridge and sump pump</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            For heater runtimes specifically, our guide to <a href="/can-a-portable-power-station-run-a-space-heater/">whether a power station can run a space heater</a> walks through the numbers, and the <a href="/best-portable-power-station-for-home-backup/">best power stations for home backup</a> ranks the units with enough capacity to matter. If you want battery heat that does not need a big station, our <a href="/best-battery-powered-heater/">best battery powered heaters guide</a> covers heated vests, hand warmers and 12V blankets.
          </p>

          <p>
            The honest summary is that electric heat on a battery is about heating people, not rooms. Use propane or a furnace for room heat during waking hours, and switch to blankets on battery power for sleeping. That combination is safe, efficient and comfortable.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="outage-plan" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. A Practical Outage Heating Plan
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start before the storm. Pick one room to be the warm room: ideally small, on the sunny side of the house, with a door you can close and few exterior windows. Hang blankets over doorways you cannot close, roll towels against the gaps under doors, and close curtains at night to hold in heat. A smaller, well-sealed space is far easier to keep at 60 degrees than a whole house.
          </p>

          <p>
            Stock fuel for at least three days. For a Big Buddy running on its medium setting for about eight hours a day, that is a 20 lb tank with room to spare. For a Portable Buddy on 1 lb cylinders, it is a lot of cylinders, which is why the hose adapter and a 20 lb tank are a smart upgrade. Store propane outdoors or in a detached shed, never in the basement.
          </p>

          <p>
            Protect the house as well as the people. When indoor temperatures fall toward freezing, open the cabinet doors under sinks on outside walls, let faucets drip and know where your main water shutoff is. If the outage looks like it will last days in hard cold, decide early whether to drain the plumbing or move to a warming center, a hotel or a relative's house rather than gambling on improvised heat.
          </p>

          <p>
            For longer and more frequent outages, the bigger fix is backup power that runs your existing furnace. A properly installed generator with a transfer switch powers the blower, the thermostat and the essentials. Our <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">best home generators for power outages</a> guide compares portable, inverter and standby options, and our <a href="/generator-transfer-switch-types-cost-and-installation/">transfer switch guide</a> explains how to connect one safely.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-to-choose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. How to Choose the Right Outage Heater
          </h2>

          <p>
            For most households, the answer is a Mr. Heater Big Buddy with a hose and a 20 lb tank, a carbon monoxide alarm with battery backup and electric blankets on a power station for sleeping. That covers daytime room heat, overnight warmth and safety for a few hundred dollars, with no permanent installation.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you only need to heat one small room, or your budget is tight, the Portable Buddy does the job for less money. Stock extra cylinders or buy the hose adapter. If you have propane plumbed to the house and outages are frequent, a vent-free heater installed to code is the most convenient choice, where local rules allow it. If you live in a rural area with long outages and are willing to follow the fuel and ventilation rules, kerosene gives you the most heat per dollar.
          </p>

          <p>
            If anyone in the house uses oxygen equipment, has a respiratory condition, or if you have infants, think hard about combustion heat at all. A power station with electric blankets, plus a plan to go somewhere warm if the outage stretches on, may be the right answer. The same applies to small apartments, where ventilation is limited and a fuel-burning heater raises CO levels faster.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever you choose, practice once before you need it. Light the heater outdoors, confirm the ODS and tip-over switch work, test the CO alarm, and check how long your fuel lasts on each setting. An outage is the worst time to read a manual for the first time.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Bringing a grill, stove or generator inside</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Charcoal grills, camp stoves, patio heaters and generators produce enough carbon monoxide to kill in minutes in an enclosed space, including an attached garage with the door open. They are never indoor heat sources. Use only heaters certified for indoor use, and keep generators at least 20 feet from the house.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Relying on the ODS instead of a CO alarm</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An oxygen depletion sensor shuts a propane heater off when oxygen drops, but it does not detect carbon monoxide or CO coming from elsewhere. Every room with a fuel-burning heater needs a CO alarm with battery backup, plus another outside sleeping areas. A plug-in alarm without a battery goes silent in an outage.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running a heater while everyone sleeps</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Even indoor-rated propane and kerosene heaters should never run unattended or overnight. Fuel can run low, ventilation can change and bedding can shift toward the heater. Turn combustion heaters off before bed and switch to electric blankets on a power station, or extra blankets and sleeping bags, for the night.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing the room completely</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Closing off one warm room is smart, but a fuel-burning heater needs fresh air to replace the oxygen it burns and dilute its exhaust. Crack a window an inch or two on a different wall from the heater. Taping every gap shut while a flame burns is how a safe heater becomes a dangerous one.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Stocking only a few 1 lb cylinders</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 1 lb propane cylinder lasts about 2.4 hours at 9,000 BTU, so a handful will not get you through a two-day outage. Buy a hose adapter with an inline filter and keep a full 20 lb tank stored outdoors. It holds about twenty times the fuel and costs far less per hour of heat.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the safest indoor heater for a power outage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For overnight use, an electric blanket on a power station is the safest, because nothing burns. For room heat, an indoor-rated radiant propane heater with an oxygen depletion sensor and tip-over switch, such as the Mr. Heater Big Buddy, is the safest fuel option when used awake, with a cracked window and a carbon monoxide alarm.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a Mr. Heater Buddy indoors?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, the Buddy heaters are certified for indoor use in most of the US. They have an oxygen depletion sensor and a tip-over shutoff. You still need fresh air from a cracked window, three feet of clearance, a carbon monoxide alarm and supervision. Do not run one while sleeping, and check local rules, since a few places restrict indoor use.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long will a 20 lb propane tank run a Big Buddy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 20 lb tank holds about 430,000 BTU. On the 18,000 BTU high setting, that is roughly 24 hours. On 9,000 BTU it is about two days, and on the 4,000 BTU low setting it is more than four days. Real runtime is a little shorter because tanks are not filled completely and cold weather reduces pressure.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What heater works without electricity?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Propane radiant heaters like the Mr. Heater Buddy, kerosene convection heaters, catalytic propane heaters and installed vent-free gas heaters all make heat with no electricity. Some use batteries for an igniter or fan. Only heaters certified for indoor use belong in living spaces, and every fuel-burning heater needs a carbon monoxide alarm nearby.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are kerosene heaters safe indoors?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They can be used indoors where local rules allow, but they demand care. They lack an oxygen depletion sensor and emit carbon monoxide and other gases. Use only clear 1-K kerosene, refuel outdoors when cool, ventilate, keep clearance, never run one while sleeping, and use a CO alarm. Some cities, including New York City, ban them.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a power station run a space heater during an outage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Briefly. A 1,024Wh power station runs a 1,500W heater for about 35 minutes, or a 750W setting for about 70 minutes. That can take the chill off a bedroom, but it drains the battery you need for phones, lights and the fridge. The same station runs an electric blanket for 9 to 22 hours.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it safe to heat my house with a gas oven during an outage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Ovens and range burners are not designed to run for hours as space heaters, and an open oven can produce carbon monoxide and use up oxygen in a closed kitchen. Many gas ovens also need electricity to ignite. Use an indoor-rated heater with a CO alarm, or move to a warmer location.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

