        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            For most homes, the best indoor heater for a power outage is a Mr. Heater Big Buddy running from a 20 lb tank through a hose and filter, used while you are awake, with a window cracked and a battery-backed carbon monoxide alarm in the room. The Portable Buddy covers a single small room for less, a vent-free heater suits homes with propane already plumbed in, and kerosene and catalytic heaters fill specific niches for people willing to follow their rules.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            At night, switch to flame-free heat: electric blankets on a power station like the EcoFlow DELTA 2 keep everyone warm without anything burning. And whatever happens, never bring a grill, stove or generator inside. If outages are long and frequent where you live, the real long-term fix is backup power that runs your furnace.
